Sometimes with form controls with numerical values you want to invalidate the field for violating min/max without snapping the out-of-range value to min or max, commonly in use-cases where NumberField holds a monetary value

Because of this, I think the native `rangeUnderflow/Overflow` validity states never get triggered as it relies on the min/max attributes on the `<input>` element (this also doesn't work: `<NumberField.Input max={50} />`)

So something like this is only possible (and as a workaround) using the `validate` function:

```jsx
<Field.Root
  name="amount"
  validationMode="onBlur"
  validate={(value) => {
    console.log("validate", value);
    if (value === undefined || value == null) {
      return "This field is required";
    }
    if (
      typeof value === "number" &&
      Number.isFinite(value) &&
      value > 50
    ) {
      return "Insufficient balance, your remaining balance is 50.00";
    }
    return null;
  }}
>
  <NumberField.Root required defaultValue={undefined}>
    <NumberField.Input placeholder="Amount in SGD" />
  </NumberField.Root>
</Field.Root>
```

